WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.867
Welcome to Module 7, Subqueries in PostgreSQL.

00:00:03.867 --> 00:00:07.617
A subquery is a query used inside another query.

00:00:07.617 --> 00:00:10.502
The important part is not the parentheses.

00:00:10.502 --> 00:00:16.642
The important part is understanding what the inner query returns and what the outer query expects.

00:00:16.642 --> 00:00:24.092
We will build this gradually, starting with result shapes and then moving through scalar subqueries,

00:00:24.092 --> 00:00:28.247
membership, existence, ANY and ALL, correlated subqueries, and derived tables.

00:00:28.247 --> 00:00:33.952
Every SQL example can run directly in the browser.

00:00:33.952 --> 00:00:38.227
You can also copy the query into pgAdmin for more practice.

00:00:38.227 --> 00:00:42.062
Keep a real project in mind as we work through this section—for example,

00:00:42.062 --> 00:00:46.357
a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up.

00:00:46.357 --> 00:00:53.312
The technical feature matters because it changes what questions we can answer reliably and how easily another

00:00:53.312 --> 00:00:56.917
analyst can understand our work.

00:00:56.917 --> 00:01:03.937
As you listen, try to connect each SQL or data concept to a decision you might need to

00:01:03.937 --> 00:01:07.417
defend later: what the data means, what one row represents, what can be missing,

00:01:07.417 --> 00:01:11.680
and what assumptions the transformation introduces.
